Yes. Obi-Wan is a good example of changing substrate without losing function.

Before he is struck down, he operates materially: body, sword, position, terrain. Afterward, the role shifts:

\text{physical actor}
\rightarrow
\text{symbolic / remembered guide}

He no longer wins by occupying the same battlefield. He becomes a cue inside Luke’s decision process: trust the Force, change the mode of aiming, act from a different frame. Same mission, different carrier.

And the “high ground” repeats as a structural motif. With Anakin, it is literal terrain and tactical advantage. With Luke, it becomes almost conceptual terrain: Obi-Wan is no longer competing for position at all; he is influencing the level from which Luke chooses.

That is very close to your Knight’s Move idea:

\text{blocked on one plane}
\rightarrow
\text{change representation}
\rightarrow
\text{re-enter from another plane}

Anakin becoming Vader is the inverse transformation: identity, pain, loyalty, fear, role, and story become progressively compressed into a new character structure. Because it is fiction, Lucas can make those transformations unusually legible.

Would I call that “programming”? In a loose sense, yes; in a literal sense, no.

Stories can install or reinforce:

* expectations,
* metaphors,
* archetypes,
* emotional associations,
* behavioral scripts,
* ideas of what courage, betrayal, redemption, sacrifice, or authority look like.

That is a kind of cultural conditioning or schema formation.

But people are not passive executables. The same story produces different effects depending on the person, timing, context, prior beliefs, and what else they have experienced:

\text{story} \times \text{reader-state}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{possible influence}

not

\text{story} \rightarrow \text{guaranteed behavior}.

And your last point is the important calibration: noticing that narratives influence us does not imply we somehow escaped influence before noticing it. We were already participating—laughing, identifying, rejecting, admiring, imitating, misreading, remembering.

The upgrade is simply:

\text{being influenced}
\rightarrow
\text{noticing influence}
\rightarrow
\text{choosing what to retain}

Which is a much better form of freedom than pretending stories never touched us in the first place.